    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4cd6e43f_1ff74b1e",
        "filename": "sdk/cc_sdk_test.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 681,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-11T12:26:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Because android was the only os type variant. The reason for using android specific logic in the input to the test was to ensure that the shared libs settings were being picked up from the shared specific section.",
      "parentUuid": "c1d17039_c7fd361c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 681,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 681,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "0107e9cf9bc0a0639481b4c16d4faf230684503d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b5e7dbb6_7875cf9d",
        "filename": "sdk/cc_sdk_test.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 681,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-11T17:49:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Because android was the only os type variant.\n\nAre SDKs implicitly limited to android OS type?\n\nDoesn\u0027t this prebuilt module need to be restricted to explicitly anyhow? What\u0027s otherwise stopping the .so\u0027s below from being used in an (admittedly hypothetical) host arm build?",
      "parentUuid": "4cd6e43f_1ff74b1e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 681,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 681,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "0107e9cf9bc0a0639481b4c16d4faf230684503d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "58fcf2a9_a03e4fda",
        "filename": "sdk/cc_sdk_test.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 681,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-11T18:15:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\"Are SDKs implicitly limited to android OS type?\" - yes. device_supported defaults to true, host_supported defaults to false. The prebuilts are the same. That will prevent the .so\u0027s being used in a host arm build. We may need to explicitly disable some os types in here (e.g. darwin and fuchsia) that may pick up android/host properties but that is a separate and general issue.",
      "parentUuid": "b5e7dbb6_7875cf9d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 681,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 681,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "0107e9cf9bc0a0639481b4c16d4faf230684503d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "148cf9a5_e0d3042b",
        "filename": "sdk/cc_sdk_test.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 681,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-12T17:16:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see. So if the cc_library rule had host_supported:true this one would output an android target specialisation?\n\nThere are tests elsewhere that tests the logic for target specialisation wrt host_supported and device_supported flags? I can\u0027t find any obvious ones in this file.",
      "parentUuid": "58fcf2a9_a03e4fda",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 681,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 681,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "0107e9cf9bc0a0639481b4c16d4faf230684503d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e9b189c3_eca869af",
        "filename": "sdk/cc_sdk_test.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 681,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-13T11:15:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The setting of host_supported/device_supported would be determined by the variants of the mynativelib cc_library which are included in the sdk which would in turn depend on the sdk\u0027s host_supported/device_supported and which native_shared_libs properties (which are arch variant) contains the \"mynativelib\".\n\nYou could look at the art-module-sdk in art/build/sdk/Android.bp. It supports both android and host and has different members in each. If you build it and look at the Android.bp file you can see how it works.\n\nThere are tests for handling target specialization around host/device_supported. Lots of tests come in pairs TestSnaphot... and TestHostSnapshot.... There are three TestDeviceAndHostSnapshot... tests, one in cc_sdk_test.go and two in java_sdk_test.go that test various combinations of them. There are also TestMultiple... tests that test multiple os tests.",
      "parentUuid": "148cf9a5_e0d3042b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 681,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 681,
        "endChar": 13
      },
